(2110056, 284054, 3569, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the comments.  This is one of those lucky shots.  It has a small flow of geese comming in and I just started shooting and this one was the best of the bunch.<br/> <br/>FYI: If you look close there are two species here.  The bottom two are the "newly split species" that used to be a sub-species of the Canada Goose.  They are Cackling Geese (see the neck band on the middle on, lighter breast, smaller bill (hard to see), and a bit smaller size than the "lesser" Canada Goose on top).  Van</td>', 'Thanks for the comments.  This is one of those lucky shots.  It has a small flow of geese comming in and I just started shooting and this one was the best of the bunch. FYI: If you look close there are two species here.  The bottom two are the "newly split species" that used to be a sub-species of the Canada Goose.  They are Cackling Geese (see the neck band on the middle on, lighter breast, smaller bill (hard to see), and a bit smaller size than the "lesser" Canada Goose on top).  Van', '2006-02-01 10:46:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(2110060, 285491, 3867, '<td valign="top">Thanks everyone who voted.  Special thanks to those who took the time to comment.  Most either loved the center comp, square crop or hated it.  I''m not a big fan of center comps myself but I experimented with everything imaginable before submitting and it just seemed to look better for this particular shot.  Thanks again.</td>', 'Thanks everyone who voted.  Special thanks to those who took the time to comment.  Most either loved the center comp, square crop or hated it.  I''m not a big fan of center comps myself but I experimented with everything imaginable before submitting and it just seemed to look better for this particular shot.  Thanks again.', '2006-02-01 10:46:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(2110152, 284949, 44242, '<td valign="top">Hi Everyone. Thanks for all the kind words. If you are interested... here are some of the out takes / other shots from the challenge:<br/><a href="//www.pbase.com/kloutit/wildlife_ii__possible_entries" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.pbase.com/kloutit/wildlife_ii__possible_entries</a></td>', 'Hi Everyone. Thanks for all the kind words. If you are interested... here are some of the out takes / other shots from the challenge://www.pbase.com/kloutit/wildlife_ii__possible_entries', '2006-02-01 11:07:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(2110155, 285745, 19936, '<td valign="top">Thanks to all for the comments.  As for the color shift I do not know how I did not noitce until after the challenge started.  You are correct that it is shifted although not as much as you might expect.  As for saturation it is pretty much as captured. Although I''m very happy with the finish I have to wonder how it would have done had I fine tuned the color balance instead of using auto.<br/> <br/>In any case I''m happy with the shot and will post a few more later. (see outtakes thread) I have one that shows the beautiful red throat and crown.<br/> <br/>Here is the shot with the proper white balance.<br/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=289665" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/19936/thumb/289665.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/19936/thumb/289665.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/19936/thumb/289665.jpg"/></a><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-02-01 13:50:52.</i></td>', 'Thanks to all for the comments.  As for the color shift I do not know how I did not noitce until after the challenge started.  You are correct that it is shifted although not as much as you might expect.  As for saturation it is pretty much as captured. Although I''m very happy with the finish I have to wonder how it would have done had I fine tuned the color balance instead of using auto. In any case I''m happy with the shot and will post a few more later. (see outtakes thread) I have one that shows the beautiful red throat and crown. Here is the shot with the proper white balance. ', '2006-02-01 11:07:42.000000', '2006-02-01 13:50:52.000000', 0),
